@ekzhu and others. Unfortunately, I do not currently have any metrics on how much this PR improves GroupChat performance. It is just more of a qualitative assessment for the most part. I did have one specific use case where I had an agent with  an advanced RAG function call to perform web research. Without send introductions, that agent never got called for group task requests that required the web research so the agents couldn’t complete the task. This was because a planner agent did not know such functionality existed within the group. Once I turned on send introductions (and tweaked the description field a bit), the web researcher agent was found by the planner and the function call was made correctly. This has worked quite well ever since.

I certainly am all in favor of alternatives to GroupChat for better task orchestration. However, given that we have it and it seems to work fairly well, esp. with gpt-4-xxx and with good prompts, it might make sense to move forward with this PR. Users can easily disable it. Also, I added an argument to send these introductions silently to avoid cluttering up the chat display.

Having said that, if this adds too much complexity esp. with the new, upcoming orchestration techniques, I would vote to not implement it. Perhaps it may be possible to allow users to override GroupChart to add the introductions in the child class (it is a fairly distinct step). If we decide not to implement this PR, I could make some better suggestions how to allow this overriding to occur.

My understanding of this is that Open AI's chat completion API accepts a function map provided in JSON schema, for example:

```json
{
  "name": "get_stock_price",
  "description": "Get the current stock price",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "symbol": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The stock symbol"
      }
    },
    "required": [
      "symbol"
    ]
  }
}
```

This JSON schema is currently specified independently by the developer, in addition to providing an implementation of the function `get_stock_price`.  In case when the implementation of `get_stock_price` changed, e.g., a new parameter is added, say `timestamp`, but developer may forget to update the JSON schema in the function map, Open AI API would still produce call to `get_stock_price` based on the outdated JSON schema of the old `get_stock_price`. So, an error will occur when the function is called at run time. 

This PR provides a way to check this error at "compile time" -- so to speak, when the Python interpreter executes the function declaration. The additional benefit of using decorator is that it is becoming the "Pythonic" way of expression function transformation/validation, as evident by FastAPI. 

I think in general improving developer ergonomic is important, so I think this PR is in a good direction. In the future we can explore other ways to prevent run time error e.g., using library like Pydantic.

