I think there will be a lot of cool use cases for customizing the speaker selection method, so definitely on the right track. What I'm not sure of is whether we should keep the implementation details in `groupchat` or not. The way I'm thinking about this problem is by decoupling the `speaker_selection_method` implementations from `GroupChat`. One way of doing it is my creating an abstract class `SpeakerSlector`.

```python
class SpeakerSelector:
    @abstractclassmethod
    def selector(last_speaker: Agent, groupchat: GroupChat) -> Agent:
        raise NotImplemented()
```

Now we can implement any type of SpeakerSelector;
```python
class RoundRobin(SpeakerSelector):
    def selector(last_speaker: Agent, groupchat: GroupChat) -> Agent:
        # Implementation details
```

Then in groupchat:
```python
class GroupChat:
    ...
    speaker_selection_method: Type[SpeakerSelector] = RoundRobin()
    ...
```

This is cool because it will allow us to implement complex selectors (graph-based for example) without making `GroupChat` "heavy" and improve maintainability. This will introduce some breaking changes but we can probably add a helper method to avoid it. cc @sonichi

