@sonichi Sorry for missing the message. Here is an example: 

```python
group_chat = GroupChat(
    agents=[agent_with_function_call, commander, agent_with_vision],
    messages=[],
)
group_chat_manager = GroupChatManager(
    groupchat=group_chat,
    llm_config=llm_config
)

chat_result = commander.initiate_chats(
    [
        {
            "recipient": group_chat_manager,
            "message": f"""
                **original_cloth_img**: <img x.jpg>
                **base prompt**: xxxxxxx
                """,
            "summary_method": "reflection_with_llm",
            "summary_args": {
                "summary_prompt": "Including the latest latest_ai_img_url, as well as original_cloth_img, latest_prompt"
            },
        }
    ]
)
```

In this case, the `agent_with_function_call` will call the tool and send results to the manager, which is then sent to the `agent_with_vision` (in this [line](https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/blob/d4aa27a21b6d20889d900539c408412ad6ae9b7a/autogen/agentchat/groupchat.py#L658)). Even if `agent_with_vision` will not call any functions, it will receive message with function calling results (aka, messages with the "tool_responses" field, from the `agent_with_function_call`). Then, the OpenAI client would crash.

You are correct that this problem is not specific to MultimodalConversableAgent. ConversableAgent with any other models (e.g., Llama, etc.) could fail similarly too. We know multimodal agent will always fail in this case regardless of which model is used, because none of them can support function calling (to the best of my knowledge).

The problem is more severe than what we exposed here.

